<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ambrosius Blarer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1492">1492</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ambrosius Blarer was a Protestant reformer from Constance who left his monastery in 1522 and led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in his home city. He also helped to reform Ulm, Esslingen, Isny and the Duchy of Württemberg, and wrote many hymns. After Constance was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and made Catholic again in 1548, he lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ambrosius-blarer.jpg" alt="Ambrosius Blarer" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rijksmuseum · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Ambrosius_Blaurer_Beroemde_hervormers_(serietitel)_Icones_virorum_nostra_patrumq._memoria_illustrium_(serietitel),_RP-P-OB-55.228.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Blarer was born on 4 April 1492 into a leading family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a>. He studied theology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, where he met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Philip Melanchthon</a>, who remained a friend for life, and after his master's degree entered the Benedictine monastery of Alpirsbach Abbey. Through letters from Melanchthon and from his brother Thomas Blarer, a student at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> from 1520 to 1523, he learned of Luther's teachings and spread them among his fellow monks, which brought him into conflict with his superiors. In 1522 he fled to Constance, though he kept wearing his habit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He began preaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a> in February 1525 and soon led the city's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> with his cousin Johannes Zwick; their brothers Konrad Zwick and Thomas Blarer sat on the city council, and Thomas later became mayor. A moral law of 1526 banned dancing, drinking, swearing and adultery. It proved hard to enforce until 1531, when all citizens had to take turns as moral guardians reporting every violation to the council. Blarer also wrote educational and religious songs sung in the liturgy, several of which are still in the Swiss Evangelical Hymn-Book.</p><p>Like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-bucer">Martin Bucer</a> of Strasbourg, Blarer drew on both Zwingli and Luther and sought a position acceptable to both, which mostly led to his exclusion from each. In 1530 Constance signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrapolitan-confession">Tetrapolitan Confession</a> together with Strasbourg, Memmingen and Ulm, the last two being cities for which Blarer was the main reformer. He kept up a wide correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Melanchthon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>, Bucer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-bullinger">Heinrich Bullinger</a>, Andreas Karlstadt and Johannes Oecolampadius, among others.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Constance, unlike the other members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>, refused to negotiate with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> unless it could keep its faith. After losing a battle at its gates on 6 August 1548, the city had to give up its Protestant faith and its status as an Imperial Free City. Blarer had fled before the battle and spent the rest of his life in Biel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a>, preaching and advising Protestants who turned to him, until his death in Winterthur on 6 December 1564. His hymns include Freu dich mit Wonn, o Christenheit.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ambrosius Blarer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1520s the city adopted the Reformation under <b>Ambrosius Blarer</b>, and the bishop moved to Meersburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-constance">Free Imperial City of Constance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Constance</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.66,9.18&amp;year=1538" aria-label="Constance on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4764 493 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ambrosius Blarer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 763h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 763h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.66,9.18&amp;year=1538">What was here in 1538? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
